	<body>
		<segment id="1" parent="129" relname="joint-list">All rise</segment>
		<segment id="2" parent="129" relname="joint-list">and give your attention to --</segment>
		<segment id="3" parent="143" relname="organization-phatic">Morning , everyone .</segment>
		<segment id="4" parent="130" relname="span">This is the time</segment>
		<segment id="5" parent="4" relname="elaboration-attribute">set for the oral argument in cause number DA22-0645 in the matter of the mental health of T. W.</segment>
		<segment id="6" parent="7" relname="attribution-positive">And I understand ,</segment>
		<segment id="7" parent="140" relname="span">uh , Miss Peterson , you 're going to reserve ten minutes for your rebuttal ,</segment>
		<segment id="8" parent="140" relname="organization-phatic">is that correct ?</segment>
		<segment id="9" parent="131" relname="span">That is correct .</segment>
		<segment id="10" parent="131" relname="organization-phatic">Okay .</segment>
		<segment id="11" parent="12" relname="topic-question">And Justice Gustafason , you can hear and see us okay ?</segment>
		<segment id="12" parent="132" relname="span">Yes .</segment>
		<segment id="13" parent="132" relname="organization-phatic">Okay .</segment>
		<segment id="14" parent="250" relname="organization-preparation">Ready to begin , counsel .</segment>
		<segment id="15" parent="16" relname="organization-phatic">Good morning</segment>
		<segment id="16" parent="250" relname="span">and may it please the court .</segment>
		<segment id="17" parent="134" relname="joint-list">My name is Kristin Peterson ,</segment>
		<segment id="18" parent="134" relname="joint-list">and I am pleased to represent appellant and respondent T. W.</segment>
		<segment id="19" parent="135" relname="span">T. W. comes today</segment>
		<segment id="20" parent="146" relname="span">asking this court to preserve the integrity of the jury system and Montana laws ' respect for those</segment>
		<segment id="21" parent="20" relname="elaboration-attribute">the state seeks to institutionalize for forced mental health treatment as a last resort .</segment>
		<segment id="22" parent="23" relname="topic-solutionhood">Miss Peterson , can you pull the mic a little closer to you ?</segment>
		<segment id="23" parent="147" relname="span">Is that better ?</segment>
		<segment id="24" parent="137" relname="span">The state can not compel a respondent</segment>
		<segment id="25" parent="24" relname="elaboration-attribute">who knowingly wishes to waive their appearance at trial ,</segment>
		<segment id="26" parent="136" relname="span">to be spotlighted by Zoom against their will for the express reasons ,</segment>
		<segment id="27" parent="26" relname="purpose-attribute">so the jury may inspect them for mental defects .</segment>
		<segment id="28" parent="151" relname="span">And a jury 's verdict can not be reduced to a mere dress rehearsal for a second successive petition and second jury trial within days of the first ,</segment>
		<segment id="29" parent="150" relname="span">where the state takes another bite at the apple on the same evidence ,</segment>
		<segment id="30" parent="29" relname="elaboration-attribute">the prior jury rejected</segment>
		<segment id="31" parent="153" relname="span">to prove the same theory as to the same person 's need for commitment ,</segment>
		<segment id="32" parent="31" relname="causal-cause">for being unable to care for her basic needs .</segment>
		<segment id="33" parent="154" relname="span">This case presents two issues for this court ,</segment>
		<segment id="34" parent="33" relname="evaluation-comment">either of which is grounds for reversal of T. W. 's involuntary commitment to Montana State Hospital .</segment>
		<segment id="35" parent="157" relname="evaluation-comment">First , the district court turned Montana law on its head</segment>
		<segment id="36" parent="157" relname="span">and unlawfully forced T. W. 's remote appearance at trial by Zoom through an unmuted and video on connection ,</segment>
		<segment id="37" parent="156" relname="span">despite T. W. 's request</segment>
		<segment id="38" parent="37" relname="purpose-attribute">to waive her appearance .</segment>
		<segment id="39" parent="159" relname="attribution-positive">Second , the district court incorrectly ruled</segment>
		<segment id="40" parent="159" relname="span">that claim or issue preclusion categorically had no impact on either A. , the state 's ability</segment>
		<segment id="41" parent="160" relname="joint-disjunction">to proceed on its second successive petition ,</segment>
		<segment id="42" parent="161" relname="span">or B. , do so by use of the same exact evidence</segment>
		<segment id="43" parent="163" relname="span">presented at the previous jury trial</segment>
		<segment id="44" parent="45" relname="attribution-positive">that had ended with dismissal on the merits , on that jury 's finding</segment>
		<segment id="45" parent="162" relname="span">that T. W. did not require commitment .</segment>
		<segment id="46" parent="166" relname="span">We will start today</segment>
		<segment id="47" parent="46" relname="mode-means">by addressing T. W. 's compelled remote appearance .</segment>
		<segment id="48" parent="168" relname="attribution-positive">As we know ,</segment>
		<segment id="49" parent="168" relname="span">this case involves back to back commitment petitions and back to back jury trials on those petitions within days of each other</segment>
		<segment id="50" parent="51" relname="attribution-positive">after the first jury found</segment>
		<segment id="51" parent="167" relname="span">T. W. did not require commitment .</segment>
		<segment id="52" parent="170" relname="span">At T. W. 's second jury trial , she made the same request</segment>
		<segment id="53" parent="52" relname="elaboration-attribute">that was allowed at her prior trial</segment>
		<segment id="54" parent="172" relname="joint-list">to waive her appearance</segment>
		<segment id="55" parent="173" relname="span">and watch trial by Zoom from a separate room</segment>
		<segment id="56" parent="55" relname="elaboration-attribute">where the video and audio were off .</segment>
		<segment id="57" parent="177" relname="adversative-concession">Although the state did not previously object</segment>
		<segment id="58" parent="177" relname="joint-list">this time , the state objected</segment>
		<segment id="59" parent="175" relname="attribution-positive">and argued</segment>
		<segment id="60" parent="175" relname="span">T. W. should either be in the courtroom or audible and visible by Zoom</segment>
		<segment id="61" parent="174" relname="span">expressly so the jury would have the opportunity</segment>
		<segment id="62" parent="61" relname="purpose-attribute">to observe her mental condition .</segment>
		<segment id="63" parent="179" relname="joint-list">The district court agreed ,</segment>
		<segment id="64" parent="179" relname="joint-list">reversed course</segment>
		<segment id="65" parent="66" relname="attribution-positive">and determined</segment>
		<segment id="66" parent="180" relname="span">the jury was entitled to inspect T. W. for mental defects .</segment>
		<segment id="67" parent="182" relname="joint-sequence">The court allowed T. W. to go to a separate room</segment>
		<segment id="68" parent="182" relname="joint-sequence">and watch by Zoom ,</segment>
		<segment id="69" parent="183" relname="span">but required her video and audio to remain on</segment>
		<segment id="70" parent="69" relname="purpose-goal">to facilitate her personal inspection by the jury .</segment>
		<segment id="71" parent="186" relname="span">The district court persisted in that order ,</segment>
		<segment id="72" parent="185" relname="span">despite T. W. 's objection and her unequivocal request</segment>
		<segment id="73" parent="72" relname="purpose-attribute">to not use Zoom at all .</segment>
		<segment id="74" parent="187" relname="span">The district court 's ruling</segment>
		<segment id="75" parent="74" relname="elaboration-attribute">forcing T. W. to remain on Zoom</segment>
		<segment id="76" parent="188" relname="span">violated Montana 's civil commitment scheme and the rights</segment>
		<segment id="77" parent="76" relname="elaboration-attribute">it granted T. W.</segment>
		<segment id="78" parent="189" relname="joint-list">to be present</segment>
		<segment id="79" parent="189" relname="joint-list">and participate in the proceeding ,</segment>
		<segment id="80" parent="192" relname="span">and waive that right</segment>
		<segment id="81" parent="190" relname="joint-list">if she so desired ,</segment>
		<segment id="82" parent="191" relname="span">and was capable of doing so under 5321 119 , and 5321 140 ,</segment>
		<segment id="83" parent="82" relname="elaboration-additional">which she undisputedly was .</segment>
		<segment id="84" parent="201" relname="span">Montana 's involuntary commitment scheme is part of a rather recent development in the law</segment>
		<segment id="85" parent="200" relname="span">to establish strong procedural safeguards for those</segment>
		<segment id="86" parent="85" relname="elaboration-attribute">facing involuntary commitment .</segment>
		<segment id="87" parent="202" relname="span">The scheme is to be administered with full respect for the person 's dignity and personal integrity ,</segment>
		<segment id="88" parent="87" relname="purpose-goal">as well as to ensure that due process of law .</segment>
		<segment id="89" parent="205" relname="span">As part of the scheme , the respondent has the right</segment>
		<segment id="90" parent="204" relname="joint-list">to be present</segment>
		<segment id="91" parent="204" relname="joint-list">and participate in a proceeding .</segment>
		<segment id="92" parent="208" relname="attribution-positive">5321 119 expressly provides</segment>
		<segment id="93" parent="208" relname="span">a capable respondent may waive their right</segment>
		<segment id="94" parent="207" relname="joint-list">to be present</segment>
		<segment id="95" parent="207" relname="joint-list">and participate ,</segment>
		<segment id="96" parent="211" relname="span">and this court has consistently interpreted 119 ( 1 ) to only confer discretion on the court</segment>
		<segment id="97" parent="98" relname="attribution-positive">to ensure</segment>
		<segment id="98" parent="210" relname="span">the waiver is knowing and voluntary .</segment>
		<segment id="99" parent="100" relname="explanation-justify">5321 140 piggybacks 119 on a person 's presence by Zoom ,</segment>
		<segment id="100" parent="213" relname="span">making it subject to the waiver provisions of 119</segment>
		<segment id="101" parent="102" relname="attribution-positive">and providing in subsection five ,</segment>
		<segment id="102" parent="214" relname="span">the respondent can choose not to appear by Zoom at all .</segment>
		<segment id="103" parent="218" relname="organization-preparation">Miss Peterson , can I ask you on section 119 ,</segment>
		<segment id="104" parent="217" relname="organization-phatic">do we have --</segment>
		<segment id="105" parent="217" relname="span">we we do n't have any case law</segment>
		<segment id="106" parent="216" relname="span">that has interpreted the facts</segment>
		<segment id="107" parent="106" relname="elaboration-attribute">that we have here .</segment>
		<segment id="108" parent="109" relname="organization-phatic">We do n't --</segment>
		<segment id="109" parent="220" relname="span">we have n't seen that lax scenario and the implication of 119 .</segment>
		<segment id="110" parent="235" relname="adversative-concession">That is true , Your Honor .</segment>
		<segment id="111" parent="222" relname="span">This court has consistently interpreted it in such a way</segment>
		<segment id="112" parent="113" relname="attribution-positive">that , um , that the court 's only discretion is to ensure</segment>
		<segment id="113" parent="223" relname="span">that the waiver is knowing and voluntary</segment>
		<segment id="114" parent="227" relname="evaluation-comment">and relevant to this in this regard is this court 's decision in in re PAC , a 2013 decision ,</segment>
		<segment id="115" parent="116" relname="attribution-positive">in which case the respondent 's attorney represented</segment>
		<segment id="116" parent="224" relname="span">that the respondent did n't wish to be present ,</segment>
		<segment id="117" parent="225" relname="span">and the court reversed that</segment>
		<segment id="118" parent="226" relname="span">because there was not a sufficient record</segment>
		<segment id="119" parent="118" relname="elaboration-attribute">that the waiver was knowing and voluntary ,</segment>
		<segment id="120" parent="228" relname="attribution-positive">but made sure to state very clearly</segment>
		<segment id="121" parent="228" relname="span">that obviously there is no reason</segment>
		<segment id="122" parent="232" relname="span">to require a respondent like PAC to attend the hearing</segment>
		<segment id="123" parent="230" relname="attribution-positive">before her attorney can inform the court</segment>
		<segment id="124" parent="230" relname="span">that she wishes to waive her right</segment>
		<segment id="125" parent="124" relname="purpose-attribute">to be present .</segment>
		<segment id="126" parent="139" relname="joint-list">Such a requirement would elevate form over substance ,</segment>
		<segment id="127" parent="138" relname="span">and would be a disservice to the respondent</segment>
		<segment id="128" parent="127" relname="elaboration-attribute">who does not wish to attend .</segment>
